t\t<p class=\"label margin-bottom-1\">APOD<\/p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"heading-22 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-2\">Astronomy Picture of the Day<\/p>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3 media-detail-hero__subtext\">\n\t\t\t\t<a href=\"\/apod\/archive\/\">Discover the cosmos!<\/a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\t\t\t<\/p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"media-detail-hero__media margin-bottom-6\">\n\t\t\t\t<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-full\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit \"><a href=\"https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=1772&#038;h=1182&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1772\" height=\"1182\" src=\"https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=1772&amp;h=1182&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"A spiral galaxy viewed face-on with an extended spiral arm.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=1772&amp;h=1182&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1772w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=300&amp;h=200&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 300w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=768&amp;h=512&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=1024&amp;h=683&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1024w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=1536&amp;h=1025&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1536w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=400&amp;h=267&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 400w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=600&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 600w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=900&amp;h=600&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 900w, https:\/\/assets.science.nasa.gov\/dynamicimage\/assets\/science\/cds\/apod\/apod\/2026\/october\/NGC772_Robert_Eder.jpg?w=1200&amp;h=800&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1200w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1772px) 100vw, 1772px\" \/><\/a><\/figure><\/div><\/div><\/div>\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<h1 class=\"display-48 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3\">Arp 78: Peculiar Galaxy in Aries<\/h1>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-row media-detail-hero__content-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-0 media-detail-hero__description\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<strong>Explanation:<\/strong> <a href=\"http:\/\/ned.ipac.caltech.edu\/level5\/Arp\/frames.html\" rel=\"noopener\">Peculiar<\/a> spiral galaxy Arp 78 is found within the boundaries of the head strong <a href=\"http:\/\/hawastsoc.org\/deepsky\/ari\/index.html\" rel=\"noopener\">constellation Aries<\/a>. Some 100 million light-years beyond the stars and nebulae of our Milky Way galaxy, the island universe is an enormous 200,000 light-years across. <a href=\"https:\/\/www.nasa.gov\/image-feature\/goddard\/2019\/hubble-spots-a-curious-spiral\" rel=\"noopener\">Also known as NGC 772<\/a>, it sports a prominent, outer spiral arm in <a href=\"https:\/\/app.astrobin.com\/u\/Robsi?i=nfm6vp\" rel=\"noopener\">this detailed cosmic portrait<\/a>. Tracking along sweeping dust lanes and <a href=\"http:\/\/arxiv.org\/abs\/0810.1748\" rel=\"noopener\">lined with<\/a> young blue star clusters, Arp 78&#8217;s overdeveloped spiral arm is <a href=\"https:\/\/noirlab.edu\/public\/images\/noirlab2209a\/\" rel=\"noopener\">pumped-up<\/a> by galactic-scale gravitational tides. Interactions with its brightest companion galaxy, the more compact NGC 770 seen directly below the larger spiral, are likely responsible. Embedded in faint star streams revealed in the deep telescopic exposure, NGC 770&#8217;s fuzzy, elliptical appearance contrasts nicely with spiky foreground Milky Way stars.<br><br><strong>APOD&#8217;s email for image submissions has changed.<\/strong> Please see: <a href=\"https:\/\/apod.nasa.gov\/apod\/lib\/apsubmit2015.html\" rel=\"noopener\">APOD Submissions<\/a>.<br><strong>Tomorrow&#8217;s picture: <\/strong>a harvest\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/p>\n\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"display-none desktop:display-block desktop:grid-col-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-5 margin-top-3 desktop:margin-top-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<table class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-table width-full\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Date<\/th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSeptember 30, 2026\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/tr>\n\t\t\t\